## Why This Is Asked

Persistent memory requires crash-safe state transitions. This task tests ability to design data structures that survive power loss, with proper ordering and visibility guarantees across multiple producers and consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Persistent memory semantics and durable flush boundaries (clwb/psnc, SFENCE)
- Per-slot sequence counters to distinguish empty/full states
- CAS-based head/tail reservation and release semantics
- Epoch-based reclamation for safe memory reuse

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-enqueue (simplified)
bool pmq_enqueue(PMQ *q, void *payload) {
  uint64_t h = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[h & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != h) return false;
  memcpy(s->payload, payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, h+1, memory_order_release);
  // flush payload and seq to NVM, ensure visibility
  clwb((void*)s, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  SFENCE();
  return true;
}

// Pseudo-dequeue (simplified)
bool pmq_dequeue(PMQ *q, void *out) {
  uint64_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&q->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[t & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != t+1) return false;
  memcpy(out, s->payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, t+N+1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this design for power-failure resume without data loss? 
- How to choose N and payload size for latency/throughput trade-offs in a busy IPC path?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic memory management without dynamic allocation, a common pattern in low-latency systems.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool basics
- Free-list within block memory
- Alignment and safety checks
- Simple double-free detection (debug)

## Code Example

```c
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define N_BLOCKS 1024
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; unsigned char payload[BLOCK_SIZE - sizeof(struct Block*)]; } Block;
static Block blocks[N_BLOCKS];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  for(int i=0;i<N_BLOCKS-1;i++) blocks[i].next = &blocks[i+1];
  blocks[N_BLOCKS-1].next = NULL;
  free_list = &blocks[0];
}
void* pool_alloc(void){
  if(!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b->payload;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  Block* b = (Block*)((char*)p - offsetof(Block, payload));
  b->next = free_list;
  free_list = b;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect double-free in a release build?
- How would you extend this to multi-threaded use without locks?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of barriers, memory visibility, and race conditions in low-level concurrent code.

## Key Concepts

- Reusable barrier (sense-reversing)
- Atomic memory orders (acq_rel, acquire, release, relaxed)
- ABA avoidance via generation/sense flip
- Spin-wait considerations and _mm_pause() usage

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int N;
  _Atomic int count;
  _Atomic int sense;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int N){
  b->N = N;
  atomic_init(&b->count, N);
  atomic_init(&b->sense, 0);
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b){
  int local = !atomic_load_explicit(&b->sense, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (atomic_fetch_sub_explicit(&b->count, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) == 1){
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->count, b->N, memory_order_relaxed);
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->sense, local, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&b->sense, memory_order_acquire) != local) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}
```

