## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design crash-consistent data structures for persistent memory, requiring reasoning about durability guarantees, lock-free synchronization patterns, and recovery mechanisms after power loss.

## Key Concepts

- **Crash-consistency in PMEM**: Using CLWB (Cache Line Write Back) to flush modified cache lines to persistent media without invalidating, combined with SFENCE to enforce memory ordering and guarantee durability
- **Lock-free MPSC patterns**: Utilizing atomic operations on persistent indices to enable multiple producers to enqueue concurrently without locks
- **Recovery mechanisms**: Detecting and repairing partially written slots, reconstructing in-flight state after system restart

## Data Structures

```c
typedef struct {
    uint64_t seq;
    uint8_t payload[56];
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t head;
    volatile uint64_t tail;
} pmem_queue_t;
```

Each slot is 64 bytes (cache-line aligned), with the sequence number serving as both a validity marker and a position indicator. The head and tail indices are stored in persistent memory to survive power failures.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests low-level memory management, determinism, and fragmentation handling in tight constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool vs variable allocations
- In-block free-list approach
- Alignment and fragmentation
- Initialization, allocation, freeing, and testing

## Code Example

```c
/* Initialization and simple allocator skeleton */
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;

static uint8_t pool[4096];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  free_list = (Block*)pool;
  Block* cur = free_list;
  for (size_t i=0; i< (4096/32)-1; ++i){
    cur->next = (Block*)((uint8_t*)cur + 32);
    cur = cur->next;
  }
  cur->next = NULL;
}
void* pool_alloc(size_t s){
  if (s>32) return NULL;
  if (!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  ((Block*)p)->next = free_list;
  free_list = (Block*)p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you add support for variable-sized allocations with splitting and coalescing?
- How would you verify correctness with a tiny unit test harness on a microcontroller?

## Why This Is Asked
Real systems synchronize thousands of threads across NUMA nodes; a lock-free barrier minimizes cache thrash and jitter in schedulers and regression tests.

## Key Concepts
- Generation counters with per-thread sense flips
- Atomic arrivals and a single release owner
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
- Timeout handling and barrier reset
- NUMA locality considerations and fairness

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  alignas(64) volatile int gen;
  alignas(64) volatile int arrivals;
  char pad[60];
} barrier_t;

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, int tid, int total, int timeout_ms) {
  int g = __atomic_load_n(&b->gen, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
  if (__atomic_fetch_add(&b->arrivals, 1, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST) == total-1) {
    __atomic_store_n(&b->arrivals, 0, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
    __atomic_store_n(&b->gen, g+1, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
  } else {
    while (__atomic_load_n(&b->gen, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST) == g) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for dynamic thread counts?
- What are the trade-offs vs futex-based barriers?
- How would you test for liveness under shutdowns and timeouts?

